  "shirogav/sh19": {
   "summary_en": "Kalila says the story was told so that Dimna would see he brought this trouble on himself. Dimna answers that he means to work against the ox by cunning, lists the six ills that ruin a king, and argues that brains beat brute strength; he begins the story of a crow that destroyed a snake by a trick.",
   "status": "draft",
   "translation_en": ""
  },
  "shirogav/sh20": {
   "summary_en": "A crow whose chicks are eaten by a snake plans to blind it; a jackal friend warns that this is foolish and tells of a heron that tricked the fish into leaving their pond and was killed by a crab. He then suggests a safer plan: the crow snatches a woman's jewel and drops it near the snake, so that the people chasing it kill the snake.",
   "status": "draft",
   "translation_en": ""
  },
  "shirogav/sh21": {
   "summary_en": "",
   "status": "empty",
   "translation_en": ""
  },
  "shirogav/sh22": {
   "summary_en": "The wild animals agree to send the lion one animal a day, so that he stops hunting them. When the lot falls on a hare, he arrives late and tells the angry lion that another lion has taken his meal; he leads the lion to a well, where the lion sees his own reflection, leaps in and drowns. The animals celebrate, and the section ends with a verse (see the margin note).",
   "status": "draft",
   "translation_en": "",
   "note_for_reviewer": "Mentions the verse that is missing in Ganjoor; see arabic_translations.json"
  },
  "shirogav/sh23": {
   "summary_en": "",
   "status": "empty",
   "translation_en": ""
  },
  "shirogav/sh24": {
   "summary_en": "Three fish live in a hidden pond. When two fishermen plan to net them, the most cautious fish leaves at once, the second pretends to be dead and is thrown back, and the third, who never planned ahead, is caught.",
   "status": "draft",
   "translation_en": ""
  },
  "shirogav/sh25": {
   "summary_en": "",
   "status": "empty",
   "translation_en": ""
  },
